 \section{Conclusion}
-Training-derived quantities can both certify and exclude nearest-prototype agreement in Gaussian ridge RBF families. A class-wise residual, spectral floor, and exact penalty envelope certify complete regions $(0,t]\times[\lambda_0,\infty)$. A strict competing vote in the large-penalty limit excludes such a region and supplies a finite disagreement threshold. A signed interval bound additionally certifies local bands $[s,t]\times[\Lambda,\infty)$. The two-dataset evidence distinguishes the early loss of sufficient certification from widespread broad-width incompatibility and establishes positive local-tail coverage at large penalties. Validation comparisons locate the sufficient reversals beyond the competitive sampled penalties. Exact witness unions quantify the information added to finite-model checks, and matched-coverage selectors identify the role of class composition in certified-subset accuracy. The resulting contribution is a training-derived analysis of decision persistence with certified, excluded, and unresolved outputs. Tighter signed bounds and alternative region geometries provide concrete routes for studying the unresolved frontier.
